# Licensing

This repository carries **three** different licences. Which one applies depends
on where a file lives:

| Path | Licence | Where |
|---|---|---|
| `tools/`, `scripts/`, `experiments/` — pipeline, tooling & experiments | **MIT** | below |
| `server/` — Ritstjóri editorial server (incl. `greynir-sidecar/`) | **AGPL-3.0** | [`server/LICENSE`](server/LICENSE) |
| `books/` — translated content | **per-book Creative Commons** | below |

Repository-root configuration files (`package.json`, `eslint.config.js`,
`vitest.*`, `.gitignore` and similar) are offered under the MIT terms below.

## Pipeline and Tooling (MIT License)

The MIT grant covers the CNXML extraction, machine-translation integration,
reinjection and HTML-rendering pipeline: the `tools/`, `scripts/` and
`experiments/` directories and the root configuration files listed above.

`experiments/` is MIT for the same reason `tools/` is, and the reason is a
combination one rather than a preference: MIT tooling already depends on the
figure-text tree under `experiments/figure-text-translation/`. `tools/publish-figure-svg.js`
reads `out/translated.svg` and `out/meta.json` from it by default (the directory is
built at `:306-312` and the two defaults are at `:319-320`), and the figure-text
driver `tools/figure-run.js` lives in `tools/` beside it. Any other licence here
would create a new MIT→copyleft edge alongside the `server/` edges catalogued
below.

**The grant covers OUR code only.** `experiments/figure-text-translation/pylibs/`
is a local dependency install and is **gitignored**, so this repository
distributes no third-party *code*, and nothing below is a sublicence of anything.
Recorded for the reader who installs those dependencies themselves: the figure
**read** layer imports **pdfplumber** (MIT) and, through it, pdfminer.six,
pypdfium2, cryptography and Pillow; the read layer and the **text-strip** layer
both import **pikepdf** (MPL-2.0); the **composition** layer imports **pycairo**
(LGPL-2.1-only OR MPL-1.1); and the **SVG** writer imports **fontTools** (MIT)
and **brotli** (MIT) to subset a face to woff2. Every one of them is used as an
imported library, unmodified.

**It DOES distribute third-party FONT DATA, which is a different thing and is
stated separately because a licence file must not overstate its own scope.**
Translated figures under `books/*/media/` carry an `@font-face` whose `src` is a
base64 **woff2 subset of Liberation Sans 2.1.5**, embedded by
`experiments/figure-text-translation/svgout.py` (and, for a translated copy kept
by ruling, `refont.py`) so a reader without that face installed still sees the
figure as laid out. Liberation Sans 2.1.5 is licensed under the **SIL Open Font
License 1.1**, which permits embedding a face in a document. That licence
reserves the font names "Liberation" and "Arimo, Tinos and Cousine", so the
subset is renamed `FigIS`; each such SVG carries the font's copyright and
trademark notices and the licence text in a `<metadata>` element.
`experiments/figure-text-translation/figis.py` records the font files the subsets
are cut from, and `experiments/figure-text-translation/fonts/Liberation-2.1.5-LICENSE.txt`
is that release's licence — a third-party licence, committed so that each SVG
can carry it. The subsets are font data, not code; they are not covered by the
MIT grant above and are not sublicensed by it. *(No count is given here: derive
it with `git ls-files -z 'books/*/media/*.svg' | xargs -0 grep -la "font-family:'FigIS'" | wc -l`;
it moves whenever a figure is published.)*

**Older rendered copies under `books/*/05-publication/` may embed the same
typeface in an earlier form.** They were rendered before the rule above. Some
embed a woff2 subset of Liberation Sans 2.1.5 that keeps the font's original
names and carries no licence `<metadata>`; some embed a TrueType subset of
**Liberation Sans 1.07.4**, which is licensed under the **GNU General Public
License version 2 with a font exception**: embedding the font in a document does
not by itself make the document subject to the GPL. They are rendered output.
A copy whose figure is still in `media/` is replaced when its chapter is
rendered again; a copy no page references any more is removed by the
pipeline's pruning step. Like the subsets above, they are font data not covered
by the MIT grant. *(List the TrueType ones with
`git ls-files -z '*.svg' | xargs -0 grep -la 'data:font/ttf'`.)*

**Some of those files carry a second third-party face, from STIX Fonts.** Some
composed `_IS.svg` files under `books/` embed, beside their Liberation Sans
subsets, a base64 **woff2 subset of STIXGeneral-Regular, and of
STIXGeneral-Italic, -Bold and -BoldItalic, from STIX Fonts 1.1.0**, used to draw symbols the source figure drew in that face. The subset is renamed
`FigSym`, because the STIX Fonts 1.1.0 licence reserves the font names "STIX
Fonts" and "TM Math". STIX Fonts 1.1.0 is licensed under the **SIL Open Font
License 1.1**. Each such SVG carries the font's copyright and trademark notices
and the licence text in a `<metadata>` element. The font file the subsets are cut
from is **not** in this repository; `experiments/figure-text-translation/figsym.py`
records where it comes from and the sha256 it must match.
`experiments/figure-text-translation/fonts/STIX-1.1.0-LICENSE.txt` is that
licence document as text — a third-party licence, committed so that each SVG can
carry it, and **not** covered by the MIT grant above. Like the Liberation Sans
subsets, the STIX subsets are font data, not code; they are not covered by the
MIT grant above and are not sublicensed by it. *(No count is given here: derive
it with `git ls-files -z '*.svg' | xargs -0 grep -la "font-family:'FigSym'" | wc -l`
— drop the `wc -l` to list the files — and it moves whenever such a figure is
published.)*

**It does not cover `server/`** (which is AGPL-3.0 — see `server/LICENSE`) **or
`books/`** (which is content under per-book Creative Commons terms — see below).

> Note for downstream users taking only `tools/` (and `scripts/`) under MIT —
> parts of that tooling reach into `server/`, which is AGPL-3.0. This changes
> nobody's rights either way; it is a combination caveat, recorded so that
> anyone taking the MIT tooling on its own knows what it expects to find beside
> it. The edges fall into five classes:
>
> 1. **Optional, guarded** — loads AGPL code inside a try/catch or behind an
>    equivalent fallback; the tool works without `server/`.
>    - `experiments/terminology-consistency/measure.py:199`,
>      `experiments/mt-vintage-comparison/scripts/tooling/tcmeasure.py:199` and
>      `experiments/mt-vintage-comparison/scripts/critic/measure_copy.py:199` read
>      `server/lib/houseStyleTerms.js` from git history at a chosen ref
>      (`git show <ref>:<path>`) and run it under `node`, in a child process, to get
>      the house-style rulings. If the file is absent at that ref, or `node` fails,
>      they return no rulings and run on with those fields empty. They need the
>      file in the repository's history, not in the working tree.
>      `experiments/d11-glossary-test/analyse.py:95` reaches the same file through
>      `measure.py`, which it imports unchanged and feeds the source text.
>
>    *(`tools/api-translate.js` → `server/services/pipelineStatusService.js` sat
>    here until 2026-11-08, cited at `:1768` though the require had drifted to
>    `:2758`. Its `--update-status` flag loaded the service to mark a chapter's
>    `mtOutput` stage complete. The require was removed that day, and the flag now
>    refuses rather than being dropped silently. The Python entries were added
>    the same day: no census before then had scanned Python.)*
> 2. **Required, unguarded** — loads AGPL code, with no try/catch to fall back
>    on. Split by *when* it loads, because that is what decides whether the tool
>    runs at all without `server/`.
>
>    **(a) At load time — the module cannot even be imported without `server/`:**
>    - `tools/validate-chapter.js:33` → `server/lib/chapterLabel`
>    - `tools/figure-run.js:78` → `server/lib/chapterLabel.js`
>    - `scripts/validate-status.js:14` → `server/lib/chapterLabel.js`
>    - `tools/generate-book-data.cjs:20` → `server/services/bookDataGenerator`
>    - `scripts/export-segment-edits.js:23` → `server/lib/chapterLabel.js`
>      (also `:28` → `server/services/segmentParser.js` and `:140` →
>      `server/lib/dbPath.js`, both lazy — but `:23` already makes the file
>      load-time)
>    - `scripts/reattach-segment-edits.js:47` → `server/lib/chapterLabel.js`,
>      `:53` → `server/lib/researchScope.js` and `:58` →
>      `server/lib/servedText.js` (also `:69` →
>      `server/services/segmentParser.js` and `:376` →
>      `server/services/segmentEditorService.js`, both lazy — but `:47` already
>      makes the file load-time)
>    - `experiments/mt-vintage-comparison/scripts/tooling/binreach.py:6` reads
>      `server/lib/houseStyleTerms.js` and runs it under `node`, in a child process,
>      at module level, so importing the script runs it, and there is no fallback.
>      It also hardcodes one machine's checkout path.
>
>    **(b) At run time, not import time — the module imports fine without
>    `server/`. Whether it also RUNS without it differs per entry, so each one
>    says:**
>    - `scripts/backfill-mt-locks.js:99` → `server/lib/dbPath.js` and `:173` →
>      `server/services/segmentParser.js` (for `chapterDir`, which
>      `segmentParser` re-exports at `:491`). Both sit inside `--db`-only
>      branches (`if (USE_DB)` at `:93`, `if (dbRows)` at `:166`). The script's
>      own comment gives the reason: the requires are lazy so that a problem
>      loading them can never break the default file-only signal. **Run without
>      `--db`, this script needs no `server/` at all** — it is the only entry
>      here with a working server-free mode.
>    - `scripts/backfill-appendix-sections.js:198` → `server/lib/dbPath.js` and
>      `:201` → `server/services/bookRegistration.js`, both inside
>      `runBackfill()` (`:197`). Importing the module does not reach `server/`,
>      **but every way of running it does**: `runBackfill()` is the only entry
>      point, `main()` (`:285`) calls it unconditionally, and the requires sit
>      above every `--db`/dry-run branch. Only importing and never calling
>      avoids `server/`.
>    - `experiments/terminology-consistency/compile_ruling_set.py:34` →
>      `server/lib/houseStyleTerms.js`, which `house_terms()` (`:76`) `require`s
>      in a `node` child process with `check=True`. `main()` calls it on every run
>      (`:84`), so the script does not run without the file.
>
>    The `chapterLabel` imports are deliberate: it is the project's canonical
>    converter between the two on-disk spellings of the appendices chapter, and
>    the repository's own rules require that conversion to happen only through
>    that module, never reimplemented inline. Taking `tools/` alone therefore
>    needs `server/lib/chapterLabel.js` (or an equivalent) alongside it.
> 3. **Data path, not code** — resolves a path under `server/` to read files
>    from it; loads no AGPL code.
>    - `tools/validate-pipeline-consistency.js:27` → `server/data`
>    - `tools/check-source-updates.js:135` → `server/data/${book}.json`
>    - `tools/translate-chapter-titles.js:46` → `server/data`
>    - `tools/generate-index.js:149` → `server/data`
>    - `tools/docx-import.js:201-208` → a per-book JSON under `server/data`.
>      Both real books are mapped by name (`liffraedi-2e` → `biology-2e.json`,
>      `efnafraedi-2e` → `chemistry-2e.json`); `${bookSlug}.json` is only the
>      fallback branch.
>    - `scripts/generate-route-inventory.js:5` → `server/routes`, whose `.js`
>      files it reads as text (`readdirSync` at `:19`) to generate a docs
>      inventory. It requires none of them, so no AGPL code is loaded, and it
>      cannot run at all without `server/routes` present. This is the one class 3
>      entry that reads source rather than data.
>
>    Retired scripts under `tools/archived/` also write JSON into
>    `server/data/`. They are not enumerated here: they are out of the pipeline,
>    and they depend on the path rather than on anything in `server/`.
>
>    *(A `tools/openstax-fetch.cjs:70` → `server/data/chemistry-2e.json` entry
>    sat here until 2026-09-07. The file was deleted on 2026-09-04 in `b593916d`
>    and the entry was not; an enumeration keeps a dead edge alive exactly as
>    readily as it lets a live one drift.)*
> 4. **Package resolution, not code** — resolves a third-party package through
>    `server/node_modules`; loads no AGPL code. Two packages are reached this
>    way, both permissively licensed themselves — `better-sqlite3` (MIT) and
>    `playwright` (Apache-2.0) — and each lives under `server/` because the
>    repository root has no dependency on it.
>    - `tools/lib/safeWrite.js:50`, `tools/migrate-pipeline-status.js:24` and
>      `scripts/backfill-mt-locks.js:98` anchor a `createRequire` inside
>      `server/`, then load the bare specifier.
>    - `scripts/export-segment-edits.js:55` and
>      `scripts/backfill-appendix-sections.js:199` join the
>      `server/node_modules/better-sqlite3` path directly.
>    - `tools/merge-glossary.js:495` tries that path and falls back to the bare
>      specifier.
>    - `experiments/figure-text-translation/render-check.mjs:37-40` and
>      `experiments/figure-text-translation/browser-sweep.mjs:342-345` look for
>      `playwright` under the repository root's `node_modules/` and then under
>      `server/node_modules/`, building both paths with `path.join` over the
>      repository root; `render-check.mjs` then falls back to the bare specifier.
>      `render-check.mjs` is on the figure driver's path (`tools/figure-run.js`
>      spawns it); `browser-sweep.mjs` is a hand-run cross-engine instrument.
>    - `experiments/figure-text-translation/probe-img.mjs:3` imports `playwright`
>      through an **absolute** path into `server/node_modules`. It is not on the
>      pipeline: it is a hand-run rendering probe, and no tool spawns it.
>      ⚠️ The absolute path is a separate defect — it hardcodes one machine's
>      checkout — recorded here because it is what makes the edge invisible to
>      the first three search shapes below.
>
>      *(Until 2026-11-08 this entry cited `render-check.mjs:11` as a second
>      absolute import, said no tool spawned it, and did not list
>      `browser-sweep.mjs`. `render-check.mjs` moved to the lookup above on
>      2026-09-15 (`4b25b8d95`), when the driver began running it;
>      `browser-sweep.mjs` has used it since it was added on 2026-09-29
>      (`dfde5156c`).)*
> 5. **Separate process, not a load** — spawns a `server/` script as its own
>    Node process and reads its stdout. No AGPL code enters the MIT process, but
>    the tool cannot produce a verdict without `server/` present. (The Python
>    `houseStyleTerms.js` readers in classes 1 and 2 also use a `node` child, but
>    they are not here: the program that loads the AGPL module there is the MIT
>    script's own `require` or wrapper, not a `server/` script.)
>    - `tools/remt-ctx.js:368` and `tools/lib/remt-checks-glossary.js:482` both
>      run `server/scripts/check-glossary-payload.js --file … --json`. They are
>      the sync and async halves of the same loader for the re-MT battery's G5
>      check; `remt-checks-glossary.js:372` records why it is a spawn rather than
>      a require — G5 must not import `server/`, so it takes VALUES.
>      *(Added 2026-09-07. Both were already matched by the second search shape
>      below and had simply never been written down — the class is new here, the
>      edges are not, and no fifth search shape is needed to find them.)*
>    - `scripts/git-backup.sh:141` runs `server/scripts/export-terminology.js`,
>      the content-backup cron's glossary export. Unlike the entries above, the
>      backup itself runs without `server/`: a failed export is logged as a
>      warning and the content backup continues. *(Added 2026-11-08. No census
>      before then had scanned shell scripts, and its path is interpolated, which
>      only the fifth search shape below can see.)*
>    - `scripts/research-backup.sh` runs `server/scripts/research-backup.js`,
>      `research-withdrawal.js` and `research-retention.js`, each as its own
>      Node process (the research file's backup job, spec §5.17), and reads
>      `research-backup.js`'s stdout (the copy's path, the expired names). It
>      cannot back the research file up without `server/` present. Its paths
>      are interpolated (`${PROJECT_ROOT}/server/scripts`), so the fifth search
>      shape below is the one that finds it. *(Added 2026-11-08, with the job.)*
>    - `scripts/verify-research-backup.sh` runs `server/scripts/research-backup.js`
>      (`where`, `verify`) as its own Node process and reads its stdout (the
>      research file's monthly restore test). Found the same way. *(Added
>      2026-11-08.)*
>
> This list is the authoritative enumeration — update it here when an edge is
> added or removed, and re-derive rather than trusting it. **Five** search shapes
> are needed, because none of them sees the others' matches. *(This said three
> until 2026-09-07, when adding `experiments/` found two edges that all three
> missed — the note's own instruction to re-derive rather than trust it is what
> caught them. It said four until 2026-11-08, when the fifth, an interpolated
> path, turned up in a Python script and a shell script.)*
>
> - a literal `'../server/…'`;
> - a computed `path.join`/`path.resolve` (or Python's `os.path.join`) over a
>   separate `'server'` argument;
> - any string literal *beginning* `'server/'` — a whole path
>   (`'server/node_modules/…'`, `'server/data'`, `'server/routes'`) or the bare
>   prefix `'server/'` used to anchor a `createRequire`. The first two shapes
>   cannot match these: there is no `'server'` token to find and no `../`
>   prefix. `tools/merge-glossary.js:495` and
>   `scripts/backfill-mt-locks.js:98` are reachable only this way.
> - an **absolute** path containing `…/server/` — no `../`, no separate
>   `'server'` argument, and the string does not *begin* `server/`, so the first
>   three shapes are all blind to it. `probe-img.mjs` above is reachable only
>   this way. *(This said "the two `experiments/` entries" until 2026-11-08;
>   `render-check.mjs` is now matched by the second shape.)*
> - an **interpolated** path — a Python f-string `f'{REPO}/server/…'`, or a shell
>   or JavaScript `${ROOT}/server/…` — whose string begins with the interpolation,
>   so none of the four shapes above can match it. Search for `}/server/`.
>   `binreach.py` and `scripts/git-backup.sh:141` are reachable only this way.
>
> Run every shape over all the code in these trees — `.js`, `.mjs`, `.cjs`, `.py`
> and `.sh` — not over JavaScript alone. Until 2026-11-08 the census had only
> ever been run over JavaScript, which is how the Python and shell entries above
> went unlisted.
>
> And do not filter `node_modules` out of the results. An exclusion meant to
> skip files *inside* `node_modules` also hides every edge that merely mentions
> it, which is most of class 4.
>
> The shapes match many sites under `tools/__tests__/` and `scripts/__tests__/`
> as well. Those are deliberately outside this list. Test files reach into
> `server/` freely — for a temp database, a manifest, or the very services they
> exercise — but they are not part of what the tooling needs in order to run,
> which is what this note is about; a downstream user taking `tools/` and
> `scripts/` to operate the pipeline does not run them. Listing only the thinnest
> of them would be worse than listing none, because some are heavier than
> anything above: a test may execute AGPL services directly.
>
> The same holds for the instruments kept under `experiments/**/evidence/`. Each
> dated folder there holds the scripts that reproduced one past measurement, kept
> unchanged beside the record they support, and they are deliberately outside this
> list too: they replay that measurement and are not part of what the tooling needs
> in order to run. Some reach into `server/`. Most of those load `playwright` through
> `server/node_modules`, often by an absolute path into one machine's checkout, so
> they do not run elsewhere at all. At least one loads AGPL code directly:
> `experiments/figure-text-translation/evidence/2026-09-13-compose-fidelity/instruments/verify/harness.mjs`
> requires `server/services/figureReviewService.js` and a server migration to replay
> a figure-review measurement. A new evidence folder therefore needs no entry here.
> *(Added 2026-11-08, after a whole-branch review found that harness listed
> nowhere and not excluded either.)*

